{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 310,
    "dDurationMs": 4490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We live in a universe with 3 dimensions of\nspace and one of time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4800,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Up, down, left, right, forward, back, past,\nfuture."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 8640,
    "dDurationMs": 2200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "3+1 dimensions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 10850,
    "dDurationMs": 5190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or so our primitive Pleistocene-evolved brains\nfind it useful to believe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 16059,
    "dDurationMs": 5310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And we cling to this intuition, even as physics\nshows us that this view of reality may be"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 21369,
    "dDurationMs": 2970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "only a very narrow perception."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 24339,
    "dDurationMs": 5301,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One of the most startling possibilities is\nthat our 3+1 dimensional universe may better"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 29640,
    "dDurationMs": 6470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "described as resulting from a spacetime one\ndimension lower – like a hologram projected"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 36120,
    "dDurationMs": 3000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from a surface infinitely far away."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 44240,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The holographic principle emerged from many\nsubtle clues – clues discovered over decades"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 49210,
    "dDurationMs": 2440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of theoretical exploration of the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 51650,
    "dDurationMs": 5300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Over the past several months on Space Time,\nwe’ve seen those close clues, and we’ve"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 56950,
    "dDurationMs": 5300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "built a the foundations needed to glimpse\nthe true meaning of the holographic principle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 62250,
    "dDurationMs": 5570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ve moved from quantum field theory to\nblack hole thermodynamics to string theory."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 67820,
    "dDurationMs": 4829,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ve made a background playlist if you\nwant to start from scratch, and I especially"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 72649,
    "dDurationMs": 2441,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "recommend catching last week’s episode."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 75090,
    "dDurationMs": 4349,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But this is tough material, so let’s do\na review."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 79439,
    "dDurationMs": 5911,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The story started with black holes, and with\nJacob Bekenstein, who derived an equation"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 85350,
    "dDurationMs": 2640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to describe their entropy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 87990,
    "dDurationMs": 5059,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A black hole’s entropy represents the amount\nof quantum information of everything that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 93049,
    "dDurationMs": 1801,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ever fell into it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 94850,
    "dDurationMs": 6269,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This Bekenstein bound represents maximum possible\nentropy-slash-information of any volume of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 101119,
    "dDurationMs": 1191,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 102310,
    "dDurationMs": 6570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Oddly, that maximum in is proportional to\nthe surface area of that space, not its volume."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 108880,
    "dDurationMs": 5179,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That was surprising – surely the information\nin a volume of space depends on the volume"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 114059,
    "dDurationMs": 7830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "– like, 1 bit per infinitesimal voxel – not\none bit per pixel on its surface."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 121889,
    "dDurationMs": 5470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Steven Hawking confirmed the Bekenstein bound\nby calculating the amount of information leaked"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 127359,
    "dDurationMs": 4261,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by a black hole as it evaporated in Hawking\nradiation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 131620,
    "dDurationMs": 5140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "His discovery of Hawking radiation led to\nthe black hole information paradox, because"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 136760,
    "dDurationMs": 5570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this radiation was expected to erase the quantum\ninformation of everything that fell into the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 142330,
    "dDurationMs": 1120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 143450,
    "dDurationMs": 4910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But destroying quantum information would break\nthe foundations of quantum mechanics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 148360,
    "dDurationMs": 1800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hence the paradox."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 150160,
    "dDurationMs": 5050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This conundrum inspired Gerard t’ Hooft\nto show that the information of all material"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 155210,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that fell into the black hole could be imprinted\non that outgoing Hawking radiation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 161290,
    "dDurationMs": 5589,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And while it’s waiting to be radiated, that\ninformation should be encoded on the event"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 166879,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "horizon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 167879,
    "dDurationMs": 4151,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Nice solution, but new paradox."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 172030,
    "dDurationMs": 5390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Things that fall into a black hole do actually\nexperience crossing the event horizon and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 177420,
    "dDurationMs": 1620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "being inside the black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 179040,
    "dDurationMs": 4900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So the interior of the black hole has a dual\nexistence."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 183940,
    "dDurationMs": 6420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "From the point of view of outside observers,\nits contents is smeared into 2-D on the surface,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 190360,
    "dDurationMs": 5610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but from the PoV of anyone falling in they\nare definitely inside the black hole, plummeting"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 195970,
    "dDurationMs": 3499,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to their doom in full 3-D glory."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 199469,
    "dDurationMs": 5610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is the first glimpse of a holographic\nspacetime: a 2-D surface that encodes the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 205079,
    "dDurationMs": 2130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "properties of the 3-D interior."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 207209,
    "dDurationMs": 7530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "‘t Hooft along with Leonard Susskind extrapolated\nthis to proposed that not only is any surface"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 214739,
    "dDurationMs": 4931,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sufficient to fully describe the locations\nof all particles in its volume, but also the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 219670,
    "dDurationMs": 5410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "full machinery of the volume can exist on\nits surface – all degrees of freedom needed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 225080,
    "dDurationMs": 3909,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to describe the behavior of everything within."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 228989,
    "dDurationMs": 5381,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it’s one thing for this stuff to FIT\non the surface – but how is it actually"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 234370,
    "dDurationMs": 1039,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "encoded?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 235409,
    "dDurationMs": 5031,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "How does the 2-D surface store information\nabout that extra dimension?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 240440,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And how do interactions on that surface correspond\nto interactions in the volume?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 246260,
    "dDurationMs": 4809,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Leonard Susskind laid out the first steps\ntowards how this could be achieved using string"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 251080,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "theory, but ultimately it was Juan Maldacena\nwho figured out a concrete string theoretic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 257440,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "realization of the holographic principle with\nAdS/CFT correspondence."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 264240,
    "dDurationMs": 1800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But I’m getting ahead of myself."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 266320,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s ignore string theory for the moment\nand just think about how to create an extra"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 271280,
    "dDurationMs": 1360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dimension."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 272640,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s say we start with a plane – a flat,\n2-D spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 277810,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now grid it up into a lattice of cells and\nmake a set of rules about how those cells"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 283490,
    "dDurationMs": 1980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interact with each other."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 285470,
    "dDurationMs": 5970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Those rules are a field theory, the lattice\nitself is the field, and the cells are some"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 291440,
    "dDurationMs": 2030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "elementary component of the field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 293470,
    "dDurationMs": 3610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But perhaps not the smallest possible component."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 297080,
    "dDurationMs": 5179,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For now let’s just say the size of those\ncells depends on how we’re looking at the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 302259,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "grid."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 303259,
    "dDurationMs": 4951,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, the resolution of our microscope\nor the power of our particle collider."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 308210,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Probably the rules between cells – the field\ntheory – depends on this scale."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 313850,
    "dDurationMs": 5110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Focus on a very small scale and we see a very\nfine grid that interacts according to one"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 318960,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "set of rules, zoom out and we see a courser\ngrid – with cells that are the average of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 324780,
    "dDurationMs": 5270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "smaller cells, and which presumably interact\nvia different rules."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 330050,
    "dDurationMs": 3350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or so you’d think, but we’re going to\nadd something weird."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 333400,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’re going to say our field theory is scale-invariant."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 337880,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ll say the rules are the same for the\nsmall pixels or big pixels."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 343600,
    "dDurationMs": 5969,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We see scale invariance in fractal patterns,\nwhere the rules defining the structures repeat"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 349569,
    "dDurationMs": 3511,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to infinitely large or small scales."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 353080,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We also see it in string theory, which I’ll\ncome back to."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 357520,
    "dDurationMs": 4769,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A field theory with this property is called\na conformal field theory."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 362289,
    "dDurationMs": 5421,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the last episode I said that a conformal\ntransformation is one that leaves all internal"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 367710,
    "dDurationMs": 1570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "angles unchanged."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 369280,
    "dDurationMs": 2340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A conformal field theory has this property."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 371620,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, you can change the scale at every\npoint on the grid separately and not change"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 377680,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the internal angles or the shapes of the pixels,\nwhich corresponds to not changing the rules"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 383080,
    "dDurationMs": 2580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of interaction."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 385660,
    "dDurationMs": 5550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By making this a conformal field theory we’ve\nadded a symmetry –invariance under local"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 391210,
    "dDurationMs": 5079,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "changes in scale – also known as Weyl invariance."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 396289,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This adds a degree of freedom everywhere – like\na new infinite number line at each of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 403169,
    "dDurationMs": 2171,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "2-D grid points."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 405340,
    "dDurationMs": 5639,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Objects on the 2-D grid also have values on\nthat number line - they exist at a certain"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 410979,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "scale."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 411979,
    "dDurationMs": 3731,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If objects at different scales don’t tend\nto interact with each other then this new"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 415710,
    "dDurationMs": 3570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "degree of freedom behaves just like another\ndimension."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 419280,
    "dDurationMs": 6599,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our 2-D grid behaves like a 3-D volume, and\nwe can treat it like one – mathematically."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 425879,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You might say it’s not a real 3-D volume\nbecause the 3rd dimension is fake."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 433479,
    "dDurationMs": 1791,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But is it?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 435270,
    "dDurationMs": 6519,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What is a dimension but a number-line of possible\nvalues which a) exists alongside the other"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 441789,
    "dDurationMs": 6321,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dimensions but is independent of them; b)\nover which the rules of physics stay the same,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 448110,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and c) imposes some kind of locality – for\nexample, elements of that number line need"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 453690,
    "dDurationMs": 3189,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to be next to each other to interact."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 456879,
    "dDurationMs": 5371,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Crudely, this is how an extra dimension can\nbe coded in a holographic universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 462250,
    "dDurationMs": 4990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But for the details we need string theory."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 467240,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Even from the beginning string theory had\nhints of this scale invariance and dimensional"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 472680,
    "dDurationMs": 1350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "weirdness."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 474030,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The first iteration of the theory, around\n1970, tried to model the strong force between"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 479310,
    "dDurationMs": 6660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pairs of quarks – mesons – as this strand\nof gluons that behaves like a vibrating string."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 485970,
    "dDurationMs": 6409,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A nice feature is that the changing the length\nof the strand – which defines the energy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 492379,
    "dDurationMs": 3181,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the bond – doesn’t change the basic\nphysics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 495560,
    "dDurationMs": 7490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That means you can pretend string length-slash-energy\nis a separate dimension as a calculation trick."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 503050,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The weird thing is that when you write the\nquantum wave equation for the gluon strand"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 508050,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with length expressed as a separate dimension\nyou get the wave equation for a graviton – the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 514690,
    "dDurationMs": 2190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum particle of gravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 516880,
    "dDurationMs": 6399,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Which is ridiculous given the puny energy\nscale of a meson - gravitons shouldn’t exist"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 523279,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 524279,
    "dDurationMs": 3781,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This and other glitches led to string theory\nbeing abandoned as a model for the strong"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 528060,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "force."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 529060,
    "dDurationMs": 5830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it was quickly rejigged to make it a theory\nof quantum gravity, and the scale invariance"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 534890,
    "dDurationMs": 4610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the strings becoming a central feature\nof string theory."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 539500,
    "dDurationMs": 2660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Fast forward a couple of decades to the 90s."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 542160,
    "dDurationMs": 4590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We now have a several versions string theory\nthat try to explain how vibrating strings"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 546750,
    "dDurationMs": 3570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can lead to the familiar particles of this\nuniverse."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 550320,
    "dDurationMs": 5420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These were tentatively united by Ed Witten’s\nM-theory, which showed that different types"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 555740,
    "dDurationMs": 4050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of string and string theories were all related\nby dualities."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 559790,
    "dDurationMs": 5780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A duality is when two seemingly different\ntheories prove to represent the same underlying"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 565570,
    "dDurationMs": 1910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "physical reality."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 567480,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These arose from the way string size and energy\nscales could be rescaled."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 574040,
    "dDurationMs": 6549,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the strangest string duality was still\nto come with AdS/CFT correspondence, proposed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 580589,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by Argentinean physicist Juan Maldecena in\n1997."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 585709,
    "dDurationMs": 6321,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Strange because it provided the first concrete\ndescription of a holographic universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 592030,
    "dDurationMs": 5210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Maldecena imagined a set of string theory\nobjects called branes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 597240,
    "dDurationMs": 5090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These are like multidimensional strings that\ncan serve as start and end points for strings,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 602330,
    "dDurationMs": 2640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but also as spaces embedded within higher-dimensions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 604970,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Maldacena considered geometrically flat 3-D\nbranes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 610970,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These branes are extremely close together\n– basically overlapping."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 615230,
    "dDurationMs": 4930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The strings connected to these branes are\nscale invariant, so their length and energy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 620160,
    "dDurationMs": 2940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can vary without changing the physics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 623100,
    "dDurationMs": 4810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Under certain assumptions he found that the\nresulting braney structure looked just like"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 627910,
    "dDurationMs": 7540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a Minkowski spacetime of 3+1 dimensions on\nwhich their lived a field theory that arose"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 635450,
    "dDurationMs": 2170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from interactions between branes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 637620,
    "dDurationMs": 4940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In itself that field theory wasn’t stringy–\nrather it was a quantum field theory like"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 642560,
    "dDurationMs": 4790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the ones that gives us our standard model\nof particle physics – a Yang-Mills theory,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 647350,
    "dDurationMs": 2070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but with supersymmetry added in."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 649420,
    "dDurationMs": 6510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It was also a conformal field theory – a\nCFT - so it was invariant to the scaling of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 655930,
    "dDurationMs": 1870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "grid sizes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 657800,
    "dDurationMs": 5500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This quality fcame from the energy-scale-invariance\nof the strings embedded in the construction"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 663300,
    "dDurationMs": 1300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of this space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 664600,
    "dDurationMs": 3890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In good string-theorist style, Maldacena defined\nincorporated that scale factor into be a new"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 668490,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spatial dimension."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 672610,
    "dDurationMs": 2720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The 3-D space became a 4-D space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 675330,
    "dDurationMs": 5550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "While the original space was flat, the new\nspace had negative curvature – it was a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 680880,
    "dDurationMs": 4110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hyperbolic, anti-de Sitter or AdS space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 684990,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The conformal field theory in the original\nspace included no gravity, but in the higher-dimensional"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 690350,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "space it became a full quantum theory of gravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 696350,
    "dDurationMs": 2539,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is AdS/CFT duality."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 698889,
    "dDurationMs": 7741,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As with the other dualities in string theory,\nthis one was extremely useful for calculations."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 706630,
    "dDurationMs": 4149,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When interactions in the lower dimensional\nfield theory are extremely strong – we would"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 710779,
    "dDurationMs": 4541,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "say the fields are strongly coupled - then\nthe corresponding higher-dimensional gravitational"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 715320,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "structures in the higher dimensional space\nwould be weak and solvable."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 718920,
    "dDurationMs": 4780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Conversely, strong gravitational fields in\nthe higher dimensional space – like in black"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 723700,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "holes – look like a solvable configuration\nof particles in the low-D space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 729220,
    "dDurationMs": 5429,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Among other things, this provided a new resolution\nto the black hole information paradox: the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 734649,
    "dDurationMs": 5461,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "information lost in a black hole persists\nperfectly comfortably in the lower-dimensional"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 740110,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 741110,
    "dDurationMs": 6010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The techniques of AdS/CFT correspondence are\neven extended to disparate fields like nuclear"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 747120,
    "dDurationMs": 2770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and condensed matter physics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 749890,
    "dDurationMs": 5870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the more startling implication of AdS/CFT\nis that it’s the first concrete realization"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 755760,
    "dDurationMs": 1250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of a holographic universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 757010,
    "dDurationMs": 7379,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The lower dimensional CFT space is the surface\nof the AdS space because the field theory"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 764389,
    "dDurationMs": 4871,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exists where the new dimension becomes infinite."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 769260,
    "dDurationMs": 5980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s tough to imagine – so let’s go\nback to our depiction of an infinite hyperbolic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 775240,
    "dDurationMs": 3010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "space from the last episode."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 778250,
    "dDurationMs": 6780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Represent a 2-D hyperbolic space as a compactified\nmap and it has an edge – at least a mathematical"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 785030,
    "dDurationMs": 1100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 786130,
    "dDurationMs": 5310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Anyone inside the hyperbolic space still has\nto travel infinitely far to get to that edge."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 791440,
    "dDurationMs": 4060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now stack many maps to represent slices in\ntime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 795500,
    "dDurationMs": 6699,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The resulting column has a geometrically flat\nand finite surface that is a spacetime all"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 802199,
    "dDurationMs": 1570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on its own."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 803769,
    "dDurationMs": 4731,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The rules of interactions between cells on\nthe surface is a quantum field theory."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 808500,
    "dDurationMs": 4589,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But those rules translate to interactions\nin the volume – in the bulk – and there"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 813089,
    "dDurationMs": 2021,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they are a theory of gravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 815110,
    "dDurationMs": 6599,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "AdS/CFT is a hint that we may live in a holographic\nuniverse."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 821709,
    "dDurationMs": 5841,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It doesn’t represent THIS universe, because\nour universe doesn’t appear to be negatively"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 827550,
    "dDurationMs": 6209,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "curved AdS space, nor does it have 4 spatial\ndimensions as in Maldecena’s calculation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 833759,
    "dDurationMs": 5171,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there are efforts to generalize this to\na universe more like our own."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 838930,
    "dDurationMs": 6740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The question we now wrestle with is this:\na series of mathematical clues indicate that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 845670,
    "dDurationMs": 6500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our universe may be holographic – or at\nleast have a dual representation in a lower"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 852170,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dimension."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 853170,
    "dDurationMs": 3560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Can these just be crazy mathematical coincidences?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 856730,
    "dDurationMs": 7979,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Maybe, but perhaps our familiar 3+1 universe\nhas an alternative – perhaps a more true"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 864709,
    "dDurationMs": 2310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "representation out there."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 867019,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "An abstract mathematical surface infinitely\nfar from our location and from our intuition,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 874779,
    "dDurationMs": 4701,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "projecting inwards our familiar holographic\nspacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 879480,
    "dDurationMs": 3510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Before we jump into comments, we wanted to\nlet everyone know there's new merch in the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 882990,
    "dDurationMs": 1250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "merch store."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 884240,
    "dDurationMs": 4130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Including the return of our Game of Thrones\ninspired shirt the heat death of the universe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 888370,
    "dDurationMs": 1050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is coming."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 889420,
    "dDurationMs": 3370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It's a great way to support us as is joining\nus on Patreon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 892790,
    "dDurationMs": 2950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Links in the description."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 895740,
    "dDurationMs": 4529,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Last week was the warm-up to today's episode,\nin which we looked out how an infinite spacetime"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 900269,
    "dDurationMs": 2371,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can have a finite boundary."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 902640,
    "dDurationMs": 5499,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "First up: no, no psychadelics were involved\nin making that episode."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 908139,
    "dDurationMs": 2801,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The universe is just that weird."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 910940,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A few of you asked whether our percieved universe\nis just the surface of a higher dimensional"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 915820,
    "dDurationMs": 1160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 916980,
    "dDurationMs": 4979,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So that's actually the opposite of the proposition\nbehind the holographic principle, which suggests"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 921959,
    "dDurationMs": 5620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that our percieved universe is the volume,\nbut it can be encoded on its lower dimensional"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 927579,
    "dDurationMs": 1180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "surface."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 928759,
    "dDurationMs": 6001,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In AdS/CFT correspondence, the volume exhibits\ngravity via a type of string theory, while"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 934760,
    "dDurationMs": 5509,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the surface exhibits no gravity - only a quantum\nfield theory similar to the field theory behind"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 940269,
    "dDurationMs": 1691,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the standard model."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 941960,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Part of the confusion comes from the fact\nthat Maldacena's derivation is for a volume"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 946360,
    "dDurationMs": 4469,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with 4 spatial dimensions, which would have\na 3-D surface."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 950829,
    "dDurationMs": 3940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So that obviously doesn't directly correspond\nto our universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 954769,
    "dDurationMs": 5911,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there's work to generalize it to our case\nof a 3-D volume with a 2-D surface."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 960680,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Related to that, Musical Ways asks whether,\naccording to Ads/CFT correspondence, can we"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 966260,
    "dDurationMs": 6460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "say there would be no gravity on the surface\nof the (2+1)Minkowski spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 972760,
    "dDurationMs": 8080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So first - the \"surface\" in current AdS/CFT\nspacetime is 3+1. 3 spatial, one temporal dimensions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 980840,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That surface contains only a conformal field\ntheory and no gravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 987560,
    "dDurationMs": 6579,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The strange miracle of AdS/CFT is that gravity\narises naturally when you add an extra spatial"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 994139,
    "dDurationMs": 6581,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dimension, which ends up looking like the\nvolume contained by the \"3-D\" surface."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1000720,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "KI9 asks whether the things we learn from\nAdS/CFT are applicable to the universe we"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1007320,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "live in given that our universe doesn't have\nnegative curvature."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1010920,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well we don't know for sure that it doesn't\nhave negative curvature - just that any curvature"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1016800,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- negative or positive - is very weak compared\nto our current ability to measure it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1022640,
    "dDurationMs": 5260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Measurements of the geometry of the universe\nindicate flatness, but we may never know whether"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1027900,
    "dDurationMs": 5330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it's truly flat, or just flat as far as we\ncan see."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1033230,
    "dDurationMs": 3280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Several people were offended that I dissed\nChronicals of Riddick."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1036510,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I want to go on record as saying Pitch Black\nwas an artistic masterpiece."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1041610,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Realmaml summarizes my position well: Chronicals\nis the third best Riddick movie but it is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1047930,
    "dDurationMs": 2230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "still better than any Marvel movie."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1050160,
    "dDurationMs": 3280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And I'm sure saying this will cause no further\ncomments."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1053440,
    "dDurationMs": 5020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Cuallito notes that it's looking more and\nmore like Roger Penrose might literally be"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1058460,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a timelord."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1059460,
    "dDurationMs": 6740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In a separate comment Midplanewanderer states\nthat Sir Roger Penrose is an unsung wizard."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1066200,
    "dDurationMs": 4410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So apparently we can't agree on what genre\nRoger Penrose belongs to."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1070610,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Personally, I'd always thought of him as a\nJedi master - especially with all that dubious"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1075730,
    "dDurationMs": 2100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum consciousness stuff."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1077830,
    "dDurationMs": 3320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Midichlorians, microtubuals, potato potahto."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1081150,
    "dDurationMs": 7300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Anyway, perhaps we need to accept that Penrose\nis beyond genre - like if Gandalf had a TARDIS"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1088450,
    "dDurationMs": 1780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and a lightsaber."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1090230,
    "dDurationMs": 4770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By the way, if anyone feels like drawing Roger\nPenrose as dressed as Gandalf with a lightsaber"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1095000,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and a TARDIS, you would win the internet."
    } ]
  } ]
}
